{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\n\nThe configuration surface and the per-run identity exist but nothing reaches the wire: rebar\nstill sends no request headers, so a gateway cannot attribute or group gate calls. Header values\nalso sit adjacent to `provider_provenance`, which is written into verdicts and sidecars\n(`findings.py:122-123`, `completion_sidecar.py:339`), so delivery without redaction would place\noperator-supplied values into durable records.\n\n## Approach\n\nResolve `${run:...}` references and place the finished headers on the call through pydantic-ai's\n`ModelSettings.extra_headers`, by extending the dict returned from `build_model_settings`\n(`structured_run.py:338`), whose result flows to the model call at `runner.py:567`.\n\nThat seam rather than the transport: one insertion covers every provider supporting the field,\nwhereas patching transport would need edits at both `providers.py:295` and\n`anthropic_model.py:155` and still miss non-httpx providers. `build_model_settings` is already a\npure function with a purity test (`tests/unit/test_structured_run_seam.py`) and reads config\nexactly as `max_tokens` does. Headers are inert on Bedrock at the pinned 1.107.2 (`models/\nbedrock.py` references `extra_headers` zero times); the `base_url` gate already excludes that\npath and the sibling docs ticket records the limitation.\n\n**Pass a fresh `dict(...)` per call, never the config-held mapping.** At the pin, the Chat\nCompletions path reads `model_settings.get('extra_headers', {})` with NO copy\n(`models/openai.py:1001`) then `.setdefault('User-Agent', ...)` (`:1002`), mutating the caller's\ndict — unlike the Responses path (`:2300`) and `models/anthropic.py:772`, which copy first.\nVerified live here: a supplied `{\"x-corp-tag\": \"rebar\"}` came back carrying `User-Agent`. This\nis upstream issue 6866, fixed by PR 6868 (\"Copy `extra_headers` before `setdefault`\") after this\npin, so the copy is the same remedy upstream landed. Handing over the config's own dict would\npollute it across calls and leak a pydantic-ai version string into the recorded header-name set,\nmaking a signed verdict's provenance non-deterministic. This is the exact path `base_url`\nselects, so the copy is required, not defensive style.\n\nHeaders attach only when `cfg.base_url` is set. A configured intermediary is the only topology\nin which they mean anything — verified inert when sent directly to `api.anthropic.com` — and\ngating prevents an operator's internal correlation values reaching a public provider endpoint.\nThis reuses the condition `capabilities.py:554` already treats as \"an intermediary is present\"\nwhen it flips provenance `tier` to `best_effort`.\n\n**An absent `${run:...}` substituend omits the whole header.** Only two scopes mint a run\nidentity — `workflow/gate_dispatch.py:167` and `:762`. `review-code` opens no `gate_config`\nscope and has no ticket, so those values are absent there, as for any standalone op. Such a\nheader is dropped rather than sent empty: an empty correlation header pollutes the gateway, and\nraising would break every standalone op merely because headers are configured globally —\nobservability must never break an operation, the rule `tracing.py` already follows. Deliberately\nasymmetric with the unset-`${env:VAR}` hard error: an env var is knowable at config time, so its\nabsence is an operator mistake, whereas a run value's absence is legitimate runtime context.\n\nValidation is re-applied after `${run:...}` substitution: those references resolve at call time,\nso a value that passed config validation is not yet proven safe. Re-running the same name/value\nchecks on the finished header closes the validate-before-substitute gap here too.\n\nRedaction: header names are recorded, values never are. `provider_provenance` gains at most the\nsorted list of configured header names, and every log or diagnostic path renders values as a\nfixed placeholder.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/llm/structured_run.py` — add `extra_headers` to the assembled model settings.\n- `src/rebar/llm/headers.py` — `${run:...}` resolution against the run identity.\n- `src/rebar/llm/capabilities.py` — record header names, never values, in provenance.\n- `tests/unit/test_header_delivery.py` (new) — the tests below.\n\n## Testing\n\nTier: a contract test driving the real assembly seam and capturing what it hands the boundary,\nplus in-process unit tests for resolution. The correlation assertion must capture headers from\nthe real call site — the dict `build_model_settings` actually returns — rather than recomposing\nthe resolution helpers, because a re-derived oracle stays green if the call site later stops\ncalling them, which is the only regression the test exists to catch.\n\n- Happy path: with `base_url` and a `${run:trace_id}` header configured, the returned settings\n  carry the resolved value.\n- Edge: with no `base_url`, no `extra_headers` key at all; with no headers configured, the\n  settings dict is byte-identical to today's.\n- Failure: an unknown `${run:bogus}` raises `LLMConfigError` naming the key; a header whose value\n  only becomes unsafe after `${run:...}` substitution is rejected.\n- Omission: a header carrying `${run:...}` is absent outside a gate run while a sibling literal\n  header is still present.\n- Redaction: a sentinel value is absent from captured log output and from the serialized\n  `provider_provenance`, while its header name is present.\n- Real-request paths: the non-mutation and merge-precedence criteria cannot be proven from\n  `build_model_settings`' return value — they need an actual request. Both drive a model call\n  through the `httpx.AsyncHTTPTransport` intercept the epic's spike already used, asserting on\n  the headers the transport observed.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] With `base_url` set and headers configured, the dict returned by `build_model_settings`\n      contains `extra_headers` with resolved values; checked by calling the real function inside\n      a gate-run scope and asserting on its return value, not on recomposed helpers.\n- [ ] Every call in one gate run carries the same `${run:trace_id}` value and two runs differ;\n      checked by capturing the settings dict from repeated calls in one scope and a second.\n- [ ] With `base_url` unset, no `extra_headers` key is present.\n- [ ] With no headers configured, the returned dict equals the dict produced before this change;\n      checked against an explicitly constructed expected dict.\n- [ ] A header carrying `${run:...}` is omitted entirely outside a gate run rather than sent\n      empty; checked by resolving headers for a standalone op and asserting the key is absent\n      while a sibling literal header is present.\n- [ ] An unknown `${run:...}` key raises `LLMConfigError` naming the offending key.\n- [ ] A header whose value only becomes unsafe after `${run:...}` substitution is rejected with\n      `LLMConfigError`; checked by forcing a run value containing CR, LF or NUL.\n- [ ] The configured headers mapping is not mutated by a model call; checked by asserting the\n      config's dict is byte-identical before and after a request driven through the\n      `httpx.AsyncHTTPTransport` intercept, which fails against the pinned upstream no-copy path\n      if the copy is dropped.\n- [ ] A configured header overrides a same-named client default rather than being dropped;\n      checked by asserting the configured value reaches the intercepted request, pinning the\n      merge precedence that is the most-regressed behaviour in comparable projects.\n- [ ] A sentinel header value appears in no log record and in no serialized\n      `provider_provenance`, while the header name does appear."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787725359453377001,"uuid":"88a30cb7-832f-4e17-8279-ec84020f16d4"}